Merge v2.1 and rebuild persistent voices on its command scheduler

Brings the v2.1 release line (lock-free audio engine, CommandScheduler
with carry-over onto loading plays, talkback, webui, and the rest of
its 123 commits) into this branch, and re-implements the persistent
voice work from beb3932 on top of it. That work was written against
main's old inline command loop, which v2.1 replaced, so the conflicting
code was resolved to v2.1's side and the feature was ported rather than
hunk-merged.

Voice lifecycle (src/voice.rs)
- A voice is kept once a play naming it starts or a setting is stored
  on it (voice_volume, voice_speed), and lasts until voice_clear or
  reset. Each opened microphone's voice_id is registered at startup.
- v2.1's loading-play count stays: a voice exists from the moment a
  play naming it arrives. A voice held only by plays still loading is
  transient, so a play that fails, is cancelled, or whose HTTP client
  left still leaves nothing behind, as v2.1 intended. Voices created by
  a play that named no voice are transient too, so one-off plays do not
  accumulate.
- clear_voice forgets a voice; a microphone's voice stays registered
  with default settings.

Commands
- voice_speed {voice, speed, pitch_correction}: stored on the voice,
  applied to its playing full-load sounds, and carried onto plays into
  it that are still loading (PendingPlays), so the last speed or
  voice_speed to reach a play wins. A windowed voice warns and stays at
  normal speed; the setting is still stored.
- play's speed is now optional: a play without one starts at its
  voice's speed, one with its own speed overrides it.
- voice_clear {voice | "*"}: cancels matching plays still loading,
  fades the voice's sounds (10ms), forgets it, and returns its
  microphones to full voice volume.
- reset: fades everything, cancels every play still loading, forgets
  every voice, and returns each opened input to its configured volume,
  unmuted, at full voice volume, using the existing SetInputVolume and
  SetVoiceVolume audio commands. An input a talkback lease owns keeps
  its level, following v2.1's rule that an ordinary unmute is rejected
  while a lease owns the input. Inputs are addressed by their position
  among opened inputs, matching the audio thread's list. Caches and
  in-flight cache loads are untouched.
- voice_stop and voice_fade_out on an idle or unknown voice succeed
  without creating it; voice_volume always succeeds and creates the
  voice, which also covers v2.1's input-only voice case. voice_stop
  keeps the voice's settings.
- set_speed_of_matching is factored out of the speed handler and shared
  with voice_speed; one STOP_FADE_MS constant replaces the per-arm ones.

HTTP and webui
- /status and /metrics report voices (all remembered) next to
  active_voices (playing, loading a play, or fed by a live input);
  /status/voices lists idle voices and adds speed, pitch_correction and
  active. POST /voice/speed, /voice/clear and /reset.
- webui: contract types, client methods (voiceSpeed, voiceClear,
  reset), and console controls for them.

Tests (new ones written first and seen failing)
- voice.rs: v2.1's and this branch's suites combined; v2.1 tests that
  asserted a voice ends with its last sound now assert it stays, and
  new tests cover the transient rule.
- pending_plays: voice_speed carries; voice_clear and reset cancel.
- main.rs fixture: voice_speed on playing and later plays, play speed
  override, streamed-voice warning, voice_clear (one and all, mic
  voice), reset (inputs restored, talkback-owned input left alone),
  input voice registration, scheduler carry and cancellation. v2.1
  tests that expected "not found" or a voice ending were updated to the
  agreed behaviour; the failed-MQTT-command logging test now uses an
  unavailable input as its failing command.
- carry_over_property_test and the scheduler script property test:
  their one-at-a-time references model kept voices, and the property
  test gains voice_speed, voice_clear and reset ops with coverage
  counters. A play that the scheduler never starts (cancelled or
  failed) does not keep its voice; documented as a difference in
  docs/command-ordering.md.

